\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4}{\ignorespaces The local entries at the top-left corner in the identity frame, green for $a$ and orange for $\bar  a$, with the forced path steps and a cross where a cell cannot be completed. (a) The two path neighbours of the corner cell have different colours. (b) $(0,1)$ has no second path neighbour of colour $\bar  a$. (c) The neighbours of $(0,1)$ are $(0,0)$ and $(1,1)$, of colour $a$, and $(0,2)$, of colour $\bar  a$, so $(0,1)$ lies between $(0,0)$ and $(1,1)$ on the path of $a$. Then $(1,0)$ needs two path neighbours and only $(2,0)$ is left. (d) If $(0,1)$ follows $(0,0)$, then $(1,0)$ lies on the path of $\bar  a$ through $(2,0)$ and $(1,1)$, and $(0,1)$ has no second path neighbour of colour $a$ (the other case is the transpose). (e) Here $k=1$. A step between $(0,k+1)$ and $(0,k+2)$ would join the two colours, so both paths close after three cells, while the grid has at least $2\cdot 4$ cells.}}{8}{figure.caption.12}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:local}{{4}{8}{The local entries at the top-left corner in the identity frame, green for $a$ and orange for $\bar a$, with the forced path steps and a cross where a cell cannot be completed. (a) The two path neighbours of the corner cell have different colours. (b) $(0,1)$ has no second path neighbour of colour $\bar a$. (c) The neighbours of $(0,1)$ are $(0,0)$ and $(1,1)$, of colour $a$, and $(0,2)$, of colour $\bar a$, so $(0,1)$ lies between $(0,0)$ and $(1,1)$ on the path of $a$. Then $(1,0)$ needs two path neighbours and only $(2,0)$ is left. (d) If $(0,1)$ follows $(0,0)$, then $(1,0)$ lies on the path of $\bar a$ through $(2,0)$ and $(1,1)$, and $(0,1)$ has no second path neighbour of colour $a$ (the other case is the transpose). (e) Here $k=1$. A step between $(0,k+1)$ and $(0,k+2)$ would join the two colours, so both paths close after three cells, while the grid has at least $2\cdot 4$ cells}{figure.caption.12}{}}
